Bug_Details:
Reported as "Alloc May Be Negative" by bufferoverrun.

malloc may be called with a negative value. For example, int n = b ? 3 : -5; malloc(n); generates INFERBO_ALLOC_MAY_BE_NEGATIVE on malloc(n).

Action: Fix the size argument or add a bound checking, e.g. if (n > 0) { malloc(n); }.

Java Example:

class JavaAllocNegativeSizeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IntegerOverflowU5Example example = new IntegerOverflowU5Example();
        example.demonstrateU5Overflow();
    }

    public void demonstrateU5Overflow() {
        int[] buffer = new int[50];

        for (int outer = 0; outer < 3; outer++) {
            int baseValue = Integer.MAX_VALUE - 1000;

            for (int inner = 0; inner < 2; inner++) {
                if (outer > 0) {
                    int step1 = baseValue + 500;

                    if (inner > 0) {
                        int step2 = step1 + 600;

                        int index = (step2 >>> 1) % buffer.length; 
                        buffer[index] = outer + inner;

                        System.out.println("Step2 (overflowed): " + step2);
                        System.out.println("Index used: " + index);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void complexUpperBoundCalculation() {
        int limit = Integer.MAX_VALUE - 10;
        int increment = 15;

        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
                if (i + j > 0) {
                    int result = limit + increment;

                    // Use in array allocation
                    int size = Math.abs(result) % 50;
                    int[] tempArray = new int[size]; //BUG: INFERBO_ALLOC_MAY_BE_NEGATIVE

                    System.out.println("Result: " + result + ", Array size: " + size);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

